# Clean radio shutdown: the "wedge until replug" is fixed (2026-10-31 02:24-02:35)

## the defect
The supervisor stopped children with Process.Kill() = TerminateProcess. For the radio that
means dying in the middle of a libusb transfer; the next bring-up then either reported
"no device 0bda:c820" or blocked inside libusb (0.03 s CPU for minutes, no log output),
and only a physical replug recovered. Hit four times tonight.

## what was tried, in order
1. Named event Local\DropBridgeRadioStop, opened by the radio after listen(); the wait
   thread closes the listener so accept() fails and the normal teardown runs. The radio
   did react, but round 1 still ended "stopped (exit -1)" and round 2 never came up,
   so the event alone was not enough.
2. WARN-level trace around every teardown step located the block precisely:
     shutdown: accept failed; stopping rx loop
     shutdown: rx loop stopped; joining rx thread   <- never advanced
   i.e. StopRxLoop() returns but the RX worker stays inside StartRxLoop.
3. Bounded wait instead of an unbounded join: wait up to 2 s for rx_ended, join if it
   arrives, otherwise detach (radio->Stop() cancels its transfers anyway) and continue.
   stats_stop / stats_thread / writer are now declared outside the accept loop, because
   the failure paths previously left joinable std::thread objects that aborted the
   process on scope exit.

## measured result (three consecutive cycles, no replug between any of them)
cycle 1  ready 02:31:03  stop 02:32:03  "rx thread did not return in 2 s; detaching"
                                            "device stopped; releasing radio" -> exit 0
cycle 2  ready 02:32:53 (43 s)  ...  exit 0
cycle 3  ready 02:34:42 (43 s)  stop 02:35:33  "shutdown: done" -> exit 0

Before: every stop wedged the chip and cost a replug. After: stop is clean (exit 0) and
the next start reaches the data plane in 43 s with the cable untouched.

## residual risk
The RX worker is detached rather than joined, so it may still touch radio/queue while the
main thread calls radio->Stop(). Three clean cycles did not crash, and the process exits
immediately after, but this is a "good enough under time pressure" shape: the honest fix
is a cancellable RX transfer so StartRxLoop returns, which is a devourer-side question
(its RX thread is the one that does not observe the stop). Tracked as a follow-up.
